Template: hexagon/configure-now
Type: select
Choices: Use the web wizard, Configure now
Default: Use the web wizard
Description: How should Hexagon be configured?
 Hexagon can be configured now, or left empty so that the first-time wizard
 configures it from the browser. The wizard prints a one-time password to the
 service log and asks for the same values there, showing the exact callback URL
 to register on GitHub.

Template: hexagon/addr
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1:8080
Description: Listen address:
 The address the server binds. Whoever reaches this port controls the Docker
 socket, so leaving it on loopback and putting a TLS reverse proxy in front is
 the supported way to publish Hexagon.

Template: hexagon/public-url
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:8080
Description: Public URL:
 The address you open in a browser. It is what the OAuth callback and the
 same-origin check are built from, so it has to match what you actually type.

Template: hexagon/client-id
Type: string
Description: GitHub OAuth client id:
 From https://github.com/settings/developers. The callback URL to register
 there is the public URL above followed by /api/auth/callback.
 .
 Leave it empty to let the first-time wizard ask for it instead.

Template: hexagon/client-secret
Type: password
Description: GitHub OAuth client secret:
 Stored in /etc/hexagon/config.json, which is created 0600 and owned by the
 hexagon user. It is cleared from the debconf database once it has been
 written there.

Template: hexagon/allowed-users
Type: string
Description: GitHub logins allowed to sign in:
 A comma-separated list. Nobody outside it can sign in, whatever the OAuth
 application would allow.

Template: hexagon/git-user-name
Type: string
Description: Git user name for sessions:
 What commits made inside a session are authored as. Leave it empty to let
 each session inherit whatever the image provides.

Template: hexagon/git-user-email
Type: string
Description: Git email for sessions:
 The email that goes with the name above.
